  "files": [
    {
      "content": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\n#define N_ITER 10 // number of simulation iterations\n#define DT 0.01f // time step\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f // to avoid zero divisors\n\n/*\n * Each body holds coordinate positions (i.e., x, y, and z) and\n * velocities (i.e., vx, vy, and vz).\n */\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\n\n/*\n * Compute the gravitational impact among all pairs of bodies in \n * the system. This worksharing loop is encountered by every thread\n * in the parallel region in main.\n */\nvoid body_force(Body *p, float dt, int n) {\n  #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n    float fx = 0.0f; \n    float fy = 0.0f; \n    float fz = 0.0f;\n\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n      float dx = p[j].x - p[i].x;\n      float dy = p[j].y - p[i].y;\n      float dz = p[j].z - p[i].z;\n      float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n      float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n      float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n\n      fx += dx * inv_dist3; \n      fy += dy * inv_dist3; \n      fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n    }\n\n    p[i].vx += dt*fx; \n    p[i].vy += dt*fy; \n    p[i].vz += dt*fz;\n  }\n}\n\n/*\n * Read a binary dataset with initilized bodies.\n */\nBody* read_dataset(int *nbodies) {\n  int b = fread(nbodies, sizeof(*nbodies), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError reading nbodie value\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n  Body *bodies = (Body *)malloc(*nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n  b = fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError reading input values\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n\n  return bodies;\n}\n\n/*\n * Write simulation results into a binary dataset.\n */\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) {\n  int b = fwrite(bodies, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError writing to output\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n}\n\nint main(int argc,char **argv) {\n  int nbodies;\n\n  Body *bodies = read_dataset(&nbodies);\n\n  /*\n   * Keep one OpenMP team for the full simulation. Static worksharing\n   * assigns contiguous body ranges, while the implicit barriers after\n   * each loop preserve the force/integration dependencies.\n   */\n  #pragma omp parallel if (nbodies >= 32)\n  {\n    for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; iter++) {\n      body_force(bodies, DT, nbodies);\n\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; i++) {\n        bodies[i].x += bodies[i].vx * DT;\n        bodies[i].y += bodies[i].vy * DT;\n        bodies[i].z += bodies[i].vz * DT;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  write_dataset(nbodies, bodies);\n\n  free(bodies);\n  \n  exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n}\n",
      "path": "src/nbody.c"
    }
  ],
  "hypothesis": "A single OpenMP team reused across the ten simulation iterations, with static worksharing over independent bodies, can reduce parallel-region startup overhead while retaining useful parallelism for sufficiently large systems. Each body's force accumulation remains serial in the original j order, preserving its arithmetic order.",
  "notes": "The force and integration worksharing loops have implicit barriers, preserving dependencies between phases and iterations. The parallel-region threshold avoids creating a team for small body counts; all valid sizes still use the same computation. No integration sources were supplied.",
  "sources_kept": [],
  "transformations": [
    "Replaced serial outer-body force traversal with an orphaned OpenMP for loop using schedule(static), keeping each body's force accumulators private and in the original accumulation order.",
    "Created one OpenMP parallel region around all iterations and statically workshared the position-update loop, amortizing team startup and retaining the required phase barriers.",
    "Added a body-count threshold for team creation to limit overhead on small inputs."
  ]
